":8234.0,"2019-20":8579.0,"2020-21":8772.0,"2021-22":8911.0,"2022-23":9100.0,"2023-24":9518.0,"2024-25":9912.0},"prov":{"description":"Financing - COUNCIL TAX REQUIREMENT (TOTAL OF LINES 805 TO 885)","form":"RS","row":"890","col":"1"}},{"label":"Net revenue expenditure","code":"RS_netrevexp_net_exp","by_year":{"2017-18":14606.0,"2018-19":13404.0,"2019-20":15407.0,"2020-21":18169.0,"2021-22":9929.0,"2022-23":17790.0,"2023-24":23158.0,"2024-25":15856.0},"prov":{"description":"NET current expenditure - NET REVENUE EXPENDITURE (TOTAL OF LINES 800 TO 804)","form":"RS","row":"805","col":"1"}}]}],"context":[{"label":"Resident population","value":103324,"year":"2020 est.","kind":"int"},{"label":"GVA (balanced, total)","value":2339000.0,"year":"2023","kind":"money"},{"label":"GVA per head","value":22535,"year":"2023","kind":"gbp"},{"label":"GDP per head","value":26035,"year":"2023","kind":"gbp"},{"label":"Band D council tax (area bill)","value":2654.28,"year":"2026-27","kind":"gbp"},{"label":"Band D \u2014 council's own element","value":294.59,"year":"2026-27","kind":"gbp"}]}},"dossier":{"summary":null,"compliance":null,"documents":[],"read_documents":[],"calendar":null,"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Eastbourne's housing investment stalls as regulatory costs mount without government funding","lead":"Eastbourne Borough Council's \u00a328.4m housing new build programme is forecast to spend only \u00a316.8m this year, leaving \u00a311.7m unspent. The shortfall stems from multiple project delays: the Bedwardwell Road scheme has been re-tendered following contractor issues; two schemes funded by the government's Social and Affordable Housing Programme have experienced grant delays; and three acquisition properties are not expected to complete until the third quarter. Meanwhile, the council faces mounting financial pressure from new housing regulations. The government has not provided funding for changes required of social landlords, the council notes, even as two major regulatory regimes take effect within ten weeks: Awaab's Law Phase 2, extending mandatory hazard inspections and remedies beyond damp and mould, and the Conduct and Competence Standard, requiring staff qualifications and workforce policy overhaul. These pressures will need to be considered within the council's 30-year housing business plan.","iso":"2026-09-16","paper_title":"091 Revenue and Capital Financial Report Q1 2026-27 - Appendix 1 (Capital Programme) PDF","paper_url":"https://democracy.lewes-eastbourne.gov.uk/documents/s41510/41510.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Eastbourne halves new homes spending forecast as contractors slip and grant schemes delay","lead":"Eastbourne Borough Council's housing new build capital programme is forecast to spend only \u00a316.8m of a planned \u00a328.4m in the current financial year, a shortfall of \u00a311.7m revealed in the cabinet's first-quarter financial report. The largest scheme, Bedfordwell Road, has been re-tendered after contractor issues and the council is awaiting the new builder's forecast for spending this year. Two other schemes funded by the Government's Social Housing Acceleration Programme (SAHP) have been delayed by grant programme delays beyond the council's control and now expect reduced spending through to later years. A third scheme, three new homes under the Levelling-up Housing Fund, was delayed from last year and is not expected to complete until the third quarter. The council did not provide a total explanation for the \u00a311.7m shortfall.","iso":"2026-09-16","paper_title":"091 Revenue and Capital Financial Report Q1 2026-27 - Appendix 1 (Capital Programme) PDF","paper_url":"https://democracy.lewes-eastbourne.gov.uk/documents/s41510/41510.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Eastbourne's new build housing scheme hit by contractor and funding delays","lead":"The council's new-build housing programme within its Housing Revenue Account\u2014the separate account for a council's own housing stock\u2014is forecast to underspend by \u00a311.7m this year, representing 41% of its revised budget of \u00a328.4m. The shortfall stems from delays affecting multiple projects: Bedfordwell Road (\u00a37m) was re-tendered after contractor issues and awaits a new contractor's forecast; two government grant-funded schemes (\u00a33m and \u00a31.1m) slipped when their grant programme fell behind schedule; and a third project (\u00a31.23m) carried over from last year is expected to complete only in the third quarter. The council faces additional financial pressure from regulatory changes to social housing standards for which the government has not provided funding, with requirements beginning in November to investigate and remedy expanded categories of housing hazards.","iso":"2026-09-16","paper_title":"091 Revenue and Capital Financial Report Q1 2026-27 - Appendix 1 (Capital Programme) PDF","paper_url":"https://democracy.lewes-eastbourne.gov.uk/documents/s41510/41510.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-30","committee":"Eastbourne Borough Council Full Council","paper_title":"Appendix A - Annual Report of Audit and Governance Committee 2025-26","paper_url":"https://democracy.lewes-eastbourne.gov.uk/documents/s42216/42216.pdf","headline":"Statement of Accounts for 2024-25 signed on time after council was previously behind on statutory compliance","detail":"The Committee provided proactive support to ensure timely signing of the 2024-25 Statement of Accounts with External Auditors' Value for Money commentary, thereby bringing the council up to date with central Government's back stop arrangements. The phrase \"up to date\" indicates the council had previously not been in compliance with statutory deadlines.","category":"financial reporting","matters":"Timely signed accounts with unqualified audit opinions are essential for financial credibility. The council catching up from prior non-compliance to current compliance demonstrates resolution of an administrative governance problem.","figures":"None","quote":"Proactive support to help ensure the Council was in position to sign the Statement of Accounts for 2024-25, which included the External Auditors commentary on the Council's Value for Money, thus bringing the Council up to date with central Government's back stop arrangements.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-30","committee":"Eastbourne Borough Council Full Council","paper_title":"Appendix A - Annual Report of Audit and Governance Committee 2025-26","paper_url":"https://democracy.lewes-eastbourne.gov.uk/documents/s42216/42216.pdf","headline":"Audit Committee governance framework has gaps against CIPFA guidance","detail":"The self-assessment identified that the Committee's Terms of Reference lack explicit reference to value for money and internal audit independence oversight. The Committee has not held private meetings with auditors (planning to start in 2026-27). No formal evaluation of member skills and training needs occurred in the past two years. These gaps were identified as areas where alignment with CIPFA guidance would strengthen the governance framework.","category":"governance","matters":"Governance framework gaps in the audit committee suggest the council's oversight assurance function itself may have blind spots. A weak audit committee means weaker oversight of management performance and financial controls.","figures":"None","quote":"the review has identified this as an area where greater clarity and alignment would strengthen the governance framework.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-30","committee":"Eastbourne Borough Council Full Council","paper_title":"Appendix A - Annual Report of Audit and Governance Committee 2025-26","paper_url":"https://democracy.lewes-eastbourne.gov.uk/documents/s42216/42216.pdf","headline":"Local Government Reorganisation poses potential governance and control challenges","detail":"The Committee recognises potential governance, risk and control challenges if a new unitary authority for East Sussex proceeds. This is identified as a key focus area for 2026-27 and beyond. The Committee has already requested briefings on HR resource planning implications, indicating significant organisational change is being considered.","category":"governance","matters":"LGR represents major organisational change creating operational risk and governance uncertainty. A lender needs to understand how financial controls and management systems will function during significant restructuring.","figures":"None","quote":"The Committee recognises the potential governance, risk and control challenges arising from Local Government Reorganisation, in addition to existing \"business as usual\" pressures. Should a new unitary authority for East Sussex be progressed, this will remain a key area of focus during 2026 to 2027 and beyond.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-30","committee":"Eastbourne Borough Council Full Council","paper_title":"Appendix A - Annual Report of Audit and Governance Committee 2025-26","paper_url":"https://democracy.lewes-eastbourne.gov.uk/documents/s42216/42216.pdf","headline":"External auditor's Value for Money findings from 2024-25 deferred for detailed consideration to 2026-27","detail":"The Committee states it will undertake further consideration of VFM audit findings during 2026-27, indicating these findings have not yet been fully reviewed or acted upon by the council. This deferral means external audit concerns about efficiency and resource use have not yet been addressed.","category":"audit","matters":"Value for Money findings represent external auditor concerns about whether the council is using resources effectively. Delaying review of these findings delays identifying and addressing whatever issues the auditor identified.","figures":"None","quote":"The Committee will undertake further consideration of the substantive findings and recommendations arising from the External Auditor's Value for Money work for 2024 to 2025","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-30","committee":"Eastbourne Borough Council Full Council","paper_title":"Appendix A - Annual Report of Audit and Governance Committee 2025-26","paper_url":"https://democracy.lewes-eastbourne.gov.uk/documents/s42216/42216.pdf","headline":"External auditors identified absence of formal escalation process for overdue internal audit recommendations","detail":"Grant Thornton noted that there is no formal process requiring departments to explain and provide revised timelines for audit recommendations that are overdue. The Committee plans to implement a formal escalation process in 2026-27. The Committee previously had to petition Corporate Management Team to encourage managers to address outstanding recommendations, indicating this has been a persistent issue.","category":"audit","matters":"Absence of formal escalation for audit recommendations indicates a control environment weakness. Without documented accountability procedures, council management can defer addressing audit findings.","figures":"None","quote":"there is no formal escalation process for departments to provide written explanations and revised timelines for overdue internal audit recommendations","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-16"},"name":"Eastbourne","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-23","since_iso":"2026-08-24","distress":false,"headline":"Service income pressures: garden waste income down \u00a3163k, cremation income down \u00a3191k, multiple income collection challenges","lead":"Persistent income shortfalls across multiple services indicate either demand changes, collection challenges, or budget assumptions that are not being met; this pattern suggests income volatility rather than one-off events.","also":["61 other findings in this window"],"watch":null,"n_findings":62,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"090 Revenue and Capital Financial Report Q1 2026-27 report - FINAL","top_paper_url":"https://democracy.lewes-eastbourne.gov.uk/documents/s41539/41539.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":204,"news_drafts_total":3}}